<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shang Zhixin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1680">1680</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shang Zhixin was the son of Shang Kexi, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>'s feudatory prince in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>. He took over his father's forces and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-three-feudatories">Revolt of the Three Feudatories</a> in 1676, but submitted to the Qing again in 1677. Still suspected of disloyalty, he was ordered to commit suicide in 1680.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shang-zhixin.jpg" alt="Shang Zhixin" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wenceslaus Hollar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wenceslas_Hollar_-_The_young_Viceroy_of_Canton_(State_1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shang Zhixin was born in 1636, the eldest son of Shang Kexi, who had abandoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> for the Qing and held the title Prince of Pingnan, the prince who pacifies the south. In 1673 Shang Kexi, pleading old age, asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> to let him retire to his adopted homeland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>, and passed his position to Shang Zhixin, who took charge of the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a> province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The court, pursuing a policy of centralisation, soon decided to abolish the Pingnan Feudatory on the pretext that Shang Zhixin was difficult to control. Shang Kexi was willing to move his family back to Haicheng, but the rising of the feudatories under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geng-jingzhong">Geng Jingzhong</a> ended the plan. Shang Zhixin was ordered to return military command to his father, who stayed loyal, many of his men deserted to the rebels, and from 1673 to 1676 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> held out as a Qing fortress in rebel territory.</p><p>Early in 1676 troops loyal to Shang Zhixin put his father under house arrest, and he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a>. After Shang Kexi's death late that year, and the surrender of rebel generals such as Wang Fuchen, he went back over to the Qing. Ordered to lead troops against Wu Sangui, he made only token efforts, hoping to preserve his own forces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1679 the emperor stripped him of much of his military power. In 1680, with a Qing victory near, he was arrested, taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and ordered to kill himself; in return his family was spared from punishment, although four of his 36 brothers were executed at the time of his suicide. He was known for the cruelty of his rule, in which some of his personal enemies were torn apart by hunting dogs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shang Zhixin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With Geng Jingzhong in Fujian and <b>Shang Zhixin</b> in Guangdong, Wu at first controlled most of southern China, but his claim to restore Chinese rule was undermined by his own past.</blockquote><a href="/state/zhou-wu-sangui">Zhou (Wu Sangui)</a></li></ul></section>
